StyxWire -- building, testing and installing
============================================

(StyxWire is the continuation of hping3; the binary is "styxwire", with
hping3/hping2/hping installed as compatibility symlinks.)

Requirements
------------

  - a C compiler (GCC or Clang; C99 or later)
  - GNU make (called "gmake" on the BSDs)
  - libpcap with its headers (libpcap-dev / libpcap-devel)
  - optional: Tcl with its headers (tcl-dev / tcl-devel) for the
    scripting mode; Tcl 8.6 and Tcl 9.0 both work. Without Tcl, styxwire
    builds as a command line tool only.
  - root privileges are needed to *send/capture* with styxwire (raw
    sockets), never to build or test it, nor for --dry-run and the
    offline "styxwire build/describe/validate" commands.
  - optional: clang for the libFuzzer targets (make fuzz).

Supported/tested platforms
--------------------------

  Linux (x86_64, glibc) is the platform the build and the test-suite are
  verified on. The BSD/Solaris/macOS code paths are still present but were
  not built as part of the current work; treat them as "compiles maybe,
  untested" until they are added to the CI matrix.

Quick start
-----------

  $ ./configure            # or: ./configure --no-tcl / --with-tcl
  $ make
  $ make check             # offline test-suite, no root, no traffic
  $ sudo make install

configure options and variables
-------------------------------

  --prefix=DIR      installation prefix           (default /usr/local)
  --sbindir=DIR     where styxwire goes           (default PREFIX/sbin)
  --mandir=DIR      manual pages                  (default PREFIX/share/man)
  --datadir=DIR     Tcl example scripts           (default PREFIX/share)
  --no-tcl          build without Tcl scripting
  --with-tcl        require Tcl: configure fails if it is not usable
                    (the default, without either option, is to use Tcl
                    when a working installation is found)

  Environment: CC CPPFLAGS CFLAGS LDFLAGS LDLIBS AR RANLIB PKG_CONFIG
               TCLSH TCL_CONFIG (path to tclConfig.sh)
               HPING_OSTYPE (override "uname -s" for cross builds)

  Tcl is looked for, in this order, through TCL_CONFIG, TCLSH,
  pkg-config (tcl, tcl9.0, tcl8.6, tcl8.5), the usual tclConfig.sh
  locations and finally a tclsh in PATH. A found Tcl is only used if a
  test program builds and links against it. The test-suite has been run
  against both Tcl 8.6 and Tcl 9.0; point TCL_CONFIG at a tclConfig.sh to
  pick a specific one, e.g.
      TCL_CONFIG=/opt/tcl9/lib/tclConfig.sh ./configure --with-tcl

  libpcap is located through pkg-config or pcap-config, falling back to
  plain -lpcap. configure exits with status 1 if pcap.h/libpcap, the
  compiler, or an explicitly required Tcl are missing; the failing test
  program output is left in .configure-tmp/t.log.

  Byte order is taken from the compiler's __BYTE_ORDER__ macro, so
  cross-compiling works with any GCC/Clang; only compilers without that
  macro fall back to running the byteorder.c probe.

make targets
------------

  make                 build ./styxwire
  make check           build and run the test-suite (tests/)
  make check-live      live send/receive over a veth pair inside a private
                       user+network namespace; no real root, no physical
                       NIC, nothing leaves the host (Linux; skips where
                       unprivileged user namespaces are off). Not part of
                       "make check". See docs/LIVE-TESTS.txt
  make WERROR=1        treat compiler warnings as errors (CI does this)
  make SANITIZE=1 ...  build with ASan/UBSan; combine with check, e.g.
                       make clean && make check SANITIZE=1
  make install         install into DESTDIR/prefix (see below), including
                       the bash and zsh tab-completions
  make uninstall       remove what install created
  make styxwire-static a single statically linked binary (needs libpcap.a
                       and, with Tcl, libtcl*.a; many distributions do not
                       ship those static archives)
  make libars.a        the ARS packet library as a static archive
  make fuzz            build the libFuzzer targets (needs clang), then
                       tests/fuzz_split, tests/fuzz_apd, tests/fuzz_opts
  make bench           build and run the offline benchmark of the packet
                       engine (docs/BENCHMARK.txt)
  make clean / distclean

Installation layout and staging
-------------------------------

  make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage   # nothing is written outside DESTDIR

  sbindir/styxwire            the binary (mode 755)
  sbindir/hping3, hping2,     relative symlinks to styxwire (ln -sf:
  sbindir/hping               repeated installs just replace them)
  mandir/man8/styxwire.8      manual page
  mandir/man8/hping3.8        symlink to it
  datadir/styxwire/lib/*.htcl Tcl example scripts (only with Tcl support)
  datadir/bash-completion/    bash tab-completion ("styxwire", plus an
    completions/styxwire      "hping3" symlink to it)
  datadir/zsh/site-functions/ zsh tab-completion
    _styxwire

  Paths containing spaces are supported (they are quoted in the recipes).
  Nothing is made setuid: run styxwire as root or grant it only the
  capability it needs, e.g. setcap cap_net_raw+ep on the installed binary.
  A setuid-root install would let any local user craft and send arbitrary
  packets, so it is avoided on purpose.

Test-suite
----------

  "make check" builds tests/test_core, tests/test_waitpacket,
  tests/test_scan, tests/test_loop, tests/test_parse, tests/test_output
  and (with Tcl) tests/test_script, runs them, then runs tests/cli.sh
  (--help/--version/option validation against ./styxwire),
  tests/linklayer.sh (a pcap fixture per supported link-layer type),
  tests/install.sh (staged install into a temporary directory),
  tests/libars.sh (a program linked against libars.a alone),
  tests/rcfile.sh (the startup file), tests/completion.sh (the shell
  completions still list exactly the options the binary accepts) and
  tests/bench --selftest (the benchmark workloads compute what they claim,
  untimed). All of it runs as an ordinary user without
  opening a socket: packets are crafted in memory or read from pcap
  savefiles written by the tests themselves, and the event loop is driven
  by a fake clock.

  The one test that does open a raw socket and send is "make check-live"
  (tests/netns.sh); it is deliberately separate from "make check" and
  runs the whole live round trip over a veth pair inside a private
  user+network namespace -- no real root, no physical NIC. See
  docs/LIVE-TESTS.txt. The historical scripts in lib/regtest also send
  real packets and are likewise not part of "make check".

  make fuzz builds three libFuzzer drivers (clang): fuzz_split (the packet
  dissector), fuzz_apd (the APD parser) and fuzz_opts (the command line
  parser). Run one with e.g.
      tests/fuzz_split -max_len=2048 corpus/
  A fixed run finding nothing is not a proof of absence.

Historical notes
----------------

  Earlier versions required copying a BSD net/bpf.h on some systems; the
  header is now only included where it exists (configure checks for it).
  The "make install" of previous versions wrote absolute symlinks into
  /usr/sbin; that is what DESTDIR/prefix replace.
